  "query_preview": "..\n\nGusty east winds will continue across the region into the early\nafternoon hours tomorrow. The Lake Wind Advisory remains in place\nuntil 4 AM tonight with wave heights will further increase overnight.\n\nEnsembles show an upper trough extending eastward into eastern\nMontana, increasing chances for rainfall and mixed precipitation\novernight. Deterministic QPF forecasts have maintained both \nspatially and quantitatively this cycle, suggesting slightly \nhigher confidence in the areas of heaviest rainfall. Rain amounts \nremain between a 0.10\" and 0.30\" over most areas. Look for lesser \namounts toward the MT/ND border. No snow accumulation is expected \neven if the initial mixed precipitation falls mostly as wet snow \novernight. The low will track further east out of Montana by \nTuesday evening. \n\nAs the week progresses, a slow moving low off the coast of the \nPNW will allow for consistent southwesterly flow aloft to bring\nmoisture rich air over the region. The upper level trough begins \nto move slowly eastward by the end of the week into the weekend\nbringing returned chances for precipitation. \n\nNear to slightly below average temperatures will take the stage \nthrough most of this week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNo changes to NBM this forecast cycle.\n\nConfidence remains above average on cool and unsettled weather \nthough uncertainty remains on specific amounts.",
